"""Tighten repeated framing so even table-cell words fit the journal ceiling."""
from common import ROOT
import re
p=ROOT/'revision/review_v3/manuscript.md';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
changes=[
("Rainfall forecasts support decisions over several time horizons. Weekly rainfall helps schedule field operations, while a four-week total informs planting and water management. These products describe the same sequence of weather. Their amounts should therefore obey a simple identity: the four weeks add to the 28-day total. We call this property temporal coherence. It is present in a dynamical ensemble when all accumulations are calculated from each member's rainfall trajectory, but separate statistical calibration of the different products can break it.","Weekly rainfall forecasts help schedule field operations, while four-week totals inform planting and water management. Because these products describe the same weather sequence, the four weeks should add to the 28-day total. This property, temporal coherence, holds when all accumulations come from each dynamical ensemble member's rainfall trajectory. Separate statistical calibration can break it."),
('There are two ways to recover that identity. Bottom-up aggregation sums the calibrated weekly samples and obtains the longer periods directly. Temporal reconciliation instead combines the weekly forecasts with independently calibrated aggregate forecasts and adjusts them jointly to satisfy the sums. Both produce coherent trajectories. The value of reconciliation therefore depends on what the direct aggregates contribute beyond the calibrated weeks. We examine that contribution here.','Bottom-up aggregation recovers this identity by summing calibrated weekly samples. Temporal reconciliation combines them with separately calibrated aggregate forecasts and adjusts all scales jointly. Both produce coherent trajectories; the value of reconciliation depends on what the direct aggregates contribute beyond the weeks.'),
('These studies show that predictive value depends on the base forecasts and their dependence structure as well as the reconciliation method.','Predictive value thus depends on the base forecasts, dependence structure and reconciliation method.'),
('Together, they suggest that the usefulness of supplementary information should be tested at the location, season and forecast horizon where it is intended to operate.','These findings motivate testing supplementary information at its intended location, season and horizon.'),
('Their fixed geographical boundaries define sampling domains rather than official climate regions. The common size makes the spatial averaging procedure comparable while retaining contrasting rainfall seasonality.','These fixed geographical domains have a common size and contrasting rainfall seasonality; they are not official climate regions.'),
('Both products express millimetres of area-mean rainfall, although the gridded observation analysis and coarser forecast resolve different spatial detail.','Both express millimetres of area-mean rainfall while resolving different spatial detail.'),
('Every outer training origin has an inner held-out mean forecast. The resulting residual pool is much larger than a separate two-year calibration sample while retaining a clear separation between model fitting and residual evaluation.','Every outer training origin receives an inner held-out mean forecast, expanding the residual pool while separating model fitting from error evaluation.'),
('Only the four weekly amounts are free variables. Enumerating their 16 possible active sets solves the weighted quadratic problem, and all aggregate amounts are then calculated from those weeks. Thus nonnegativity and aggregation hold for every issued trajectory. The projection is applied in millimetres, where rainfall is additive.','The four weekly amounts are the free variables. Enumerating their 16 active sets solves the weighted quadratic problem; aggregates are then calculated from the weeks. Every trajectory is nonnegative and coherent. Projection uses millimetres, where rainfall is additive.'),
('These are small effects, but they accompany the larger northern aggregate change and demonstrate that an adjustment can distribute benefits and costs across scales.','These small changes accompany the larger northern aggregate gain and show trade-offs across scales.'),
('The additional controls sharpen the distinction between information and coherence. A larger hierarchy has little average effect, and climate-informed aggregates help mainly in the exploratory northern comparison. However, some of that information can also be introduced through the weekly marginals and retained by simple summation. In the forward northern evaluation, that simpler route has the lower CRPS. The experiment therefore supports testing where climate information enters the forecast, rather than assigning the full gain over rainfall-only bottom-up to reconciliation.','The controls distinguish information from coherence. Climate-informed aggregates help mainly in the exploratory northern comparison, while adding hierarchy nodes has little average effect. Weekly calibration and summation can also retain climate information and have lower northern CRPS in the forward evaluation. The gain over rainfall-only bottom-up therefore depends on where climate information enters the forecast and cannot be assigned entirely to reconciliation.'),
('The variance calculations have a narrower role than direct verification. Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance, whereas the issued samples undergo nonnegative projection and are evaluated with distributional scores. The optimistic seasonal gain predictions demonstrate the practical gap. Likewise, bottom-up optimality for particular temporally aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) does not determine the result for separately calibrated rainfall marginals.','Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance. Here, samples undergo nonnegative projection and are evaluated with distributional scores, for which the covariance-based gains are optimistic. Bottom-up optimality for particular temporally aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) likewise does not determine the outcome for separately calibrated rainfall marginals.'),
('Within that scope, a useful workflow is to compare rainfall-only and climate-informed weekly aggregation before adding direct aggregate models, then verify probabilities and scale-specific losses for every coherent product.','For these domains, compare rainfall-only and climate-informed weekly aggregation before adding aggregate models, then verify probabilities and scale-specific losses.'),
('Temporal coherence ensures that rainfall amounts agree across forecast horizons. Its additional forecasting value must be assessed against coherent alternatives using the information available at each scale. The new controls make that comparison explicit and support local, distribution-based evaluation of climate predictors and reconciliation.','Temporal coherence ensures agreement across forecast horizons. Assessing its additional value requires coherent alternatives using the information available at each scale. These controls support local evaluation of climate predictors and reconciliation through their predictive distributions.'),
('The archive follows the 2020 production schedule and associated model configurations; it is a retrospective forecast collection rather than a record of forecasts issued operationally during the verification years.','The archive follows 2020 production dates and model configurations. It contains retrospective forecasts, rather than forecasts issued operationally in the verification years.'),
('A seasonal EMOS model without ensemble variance is retained as a baseline diagnostic, together with seasonal empirical climatology, member-wise quantile mapping and ECMWF (QC).','Baselines include seasonal EMOS without ensemble variance, seasonal empirical climatology, member-wise quantile mapping and ECMWF (QC).')]
old=s
for a,b in changes:
 assert s.count(a)==1,a
 s=s.replace(a,b)
assert re.findall(r'\d+(?:\.\d+)?',old)==re.findall(r'\d+(?:\.\d+)?',s)
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8');print('Removed',len(old.split())-len(s.split()),'words; numerical tokens preserved.')
